  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nToday through Mon night...First round of showers is currently\nmoving through the eastern half of the forecast area at this\ntime. Light precipitation will continue on and off today and\nthis evening. The trend compared to yesterday is that it will be\nlighter amounts for the evening hours tonight, with moderate\namounts of rainfall/snowfall in the central Idaho mountains. At\nthis time, expecting 3 to 5 inches of snow on Galena Summit,\nwith heaviest amounts of snowfall on the mountain ranges on the\neast side of the Wood River Valley. Winter Weather Advisory\nstill appears to be valid and will continue. Rain amounts appear\nto be up slightly, with storm totals at Pocatello near an inch\nfor the Fri night through Mon morning period. Peak rainfall\naccumulation occurs in the Snake River plain Sun afternoon and\nSun evening. \n\nBy Mon morning only the highlands bordering WY will still be\nreceiving precipitation. Mon night will be a transition to dry\nconditions, save for the likely high humidity that will bring a\nthreat of fog, including dense fog, to the Tue morning commute. \n\nTemperatures will cool compared to Fri, and cool some more from\ntoday to Sun. This is mainly due to airmass displacement. Clouds\nwill have cleared enough in the central Idaho mountains for a\nwarming trend to develop starting Mon, but the Snake River plain\nand eastern highlands will have to wait election day.\n\nSouthern Idaho's relative postion in relation to the low's\ncenter means not a lot of wind with this storm. However, there\narea some windy conditions after the low shifts eastward in the\nsouthwest quadrant of the forecast area. \n\nTue through Fri...An upper level ridge keeps conditions calm and\nsunny on Election Day, but the breakdown of this upper level\nridge is in question. There are four different clusters of\nsolutions in this medium range period. It is about a 60-40 split\nthat the ridge will remain on Wed, then flatter, zonal flow or\nweak troughing developing Thu. Zonal flow makes timing of\nshortwave energy more difficult, so there is quite a bit of\nuncertainty in the forecast starting Thu.",
